-- A type of your own named EVENT, with two parameters, is ambiguous with the
-- builtin EVENT type, which also takes two: naming it has two candidates and
-- the checker asks which is meant. This is how every builtin type name behaves
-- (a `DECLARE MAYBE a` is ambiguous with the builtin MAYBE in the same way).
--
-- Before the builtin EVENT type could be named, such a declaration was the only
-- EVENT of two parameters in scope, so this file checked. A declaration of EVENT
-- with no parameters is not ambiguous, because the number of parameters rules
-- the builtin out; this file is about the two-parameter one.
--
-- The golden holds the ambiguity, with both candidates, and no other error:
-- the checker does not count the arguments of a name it could not resolve.

DECLARE EVENT party action
  HAS who IS A party
      what IS AN action

GIVEN e IS AN EVENT STRING NUMBER
GIVETH A STRING
f e MEANS e's who
